><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260305104347-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260305104439-05'00'" name="51ec8fa0-2398-5346-a7a0-5b48db9cb105" page="43" coords="56.693000,316.344000,555.304000,316.344000,56.693000,303.612000,555.304000,303.612000,56.693000,305.385000,121.297000,305.385000,56.693000,292.653000,121.297000,292.653000" rect="56.295100,292.255000,555.702000,316.742000" subject="Comment on Text" title="paula.fratantoni"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Are we no longer presenting in situ bottom temperature time series as submitted each year?</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="43" rect="443.129000,206.834000,627.057000,316.143000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260305104459-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260305104520-05'00'" name="d36c83f8-ce79-7446-9c1b-11f647e2708f" page="43" coords="322.350000,249.413000,555.310000,249.413000,322.350000,235.296000,555.310000,235.296000,56.693000,237.458000,555.310000,237.458000,56.693000,223.341000,555.310000,223.341000,56.693000,225.502000,555.305000,225.502000,56.693000,211.385000,555.305000,211.385000,56.693000,213.547000,555.309000,213.547000,56.693000,199.430000,555.309000,199.430000,56.693000,201.592000,372.466000,201.592000,56.693000,187.475000,372.466000,187.475000" rect="56.251800,187.034000,555.751000,249.854000" subject="Comment on Text" title="paula.fratantoni"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>I suggest rewording this as below.  Since we are not showing a figure demonstrating the decreased stability of the GS, I suggest we remove that piece. 

"The Gulf Stream has been shifted north of its long-term mean position over much of the last decade (Figure 42).  However, since 2023 the Gulf Stream has shifted southward and remained close to its mean position. With this shift, the supply of Labrador Slope Water has increased along the Northwest Atlantic Shelf.  These changes are linked to the cooler water temperatures observed in 2024 and 2025 and shifts in the composition of source waters entering the Gulf of Maine through the Northeast Channel (see 2025 Highlights)"</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="yes" page="43" rect="443.129000,-26.840000,627.057000,249.379000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260227081427-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260227081434-05'00'" name="d4f3e49a-5ff2-4291-ac45-bae6f7566e9c" page="43" coords="466.897000,316.344000,488.781000,316.344000,466.897000,303.612000,488.781000,303.612000" rect="466.499000,303.214000,489.179000,316.742000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Kristan Blackhart"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Gulf of Maine (GOM; top) and Georges Bank (GB; bottom)</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
