><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>"peaked" as you're referencing the earlier bit of the time series here</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="46" rect="588.653000,435.273000,772.580000,532.801000"
/></highlight
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20260219153349-05'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" date="D:20260225150301-05'00'" name="d7df5434-acc5-477a-a86e-0f4c8380482a" icon="Comment" page="46" rect="503.999000,274.910000,527.999000,298.910000" subject="Sticky Note" title="Cameron Hodgdon"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Why the separate trend of the recent series for Haddock GB? How were these years decided as a recent series?</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="46" rect="588.653000,190.256000,772.580000,298.910000"
/></text
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260224124051-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260224124406-05'00'" name="c4b218ab-274e-46d2-8113-bc08b73a348d" page="46" coords="162.602000,508.975000,486.418000,508.975000,162.602000,494.858000,486.418000,494.858000" rect="162.161000,494.417000,486.859000,509.416000" subject="Comment on Text" title="Cameron Hodgdon"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.4.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.6pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>I'm quite cautious stating this with certainty for SNEMA yellowtail- this observation could be an artifact of the extremely low sample sizes in the recent time series or the truncated spatial area of survey catches moving northward. Maybe a change of wording here using "could have also shifted"</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="46" rect="588.653000,344.510000,772.580000,508.801000"
/></highlight
><strikeout color="#F86464" creationdate="D:20260224124111-05'00'" flags="print" date="D:20260224124110-05'00'" name="0341a5df-f27d-4b40-981c-64d9fc59d0e0" page="46" coords="92.809600,508.975000,95.577200,508.975000,92.809600,494.858000,95.577200,494.858000" rect="88.599900,493.976000,99.786900,509.857000" subject="Cross-Out" title="Cameron Hodgdon"
