><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.5pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>are these long-term/short-term as in other plots (i.e. GB haddock)? If so, that should be clearly explained in the caption.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="yes" page="46" rect="612.129000,27.755400,796.055000,136.410000"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20260305105020-05'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20260305105308-05'00'" name="74fafadd-36f2-4848-a9c8-c367c7f0a273" page="47" coords="423.983000,473.566000,530.626000,473.566000,423.983000,459.449000,530.626000,459.449000" rect="423.542000,459.008000,531.067000,474.007000" subject="Comment on Text" title="paula.fratantoni"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>this is not demonstrated in recent years for the GOM or GB as shown in Fig 45.  Unlike the MAB, the figure indicates that the duration of ocean summer conditions is near normal in the GOM and shorter than normal on GB.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="47" rect="443.129000,331.198000,627.057000,473.889000"
